from common import *
p=paired
p('42322','A wage floor holds pay at $30 in the market shown. If it is removed with both curves unchanged, which equilibrium wage and adjustment follow?',
 '$20; hiring rises and labor offered falls along the existing curves.','$25; hiring rises and labor offered falls along the existing curves.',
 '$20; the wage decline itself shifts demand right and supply left.','$25; the wage decline itself shifts demand right and supply left.',
 'The curves intersect at $20 and 4,000 workers. Removing the above-equilibrium floor allows wage to fall toward $20, increasing quantity demanded and reducing quantity supplied along unchanged curves.',
 'Supply and demand intersect at wage $20, employment 40 hundreds.','Distinguish adjustment along curves from shifts after removal of a binding floor.')
p('42325','A proposal requires employment of 6,000 workers in the market shown, with productivity and workers’ opportunity costs unchanged. Which marginal comparison and welfare assessment are correct?',
 'Opportunity cost is $25 and product value $15; the marginal hires reduce surplus, and their wages are transfers.',
 'Opportunity cost is $30 and product value $20; the marginal hires reduce surplus, and their wages are transfers.',
 'Opportunity cost is $25 and product value $15; the wage payment itself proves that the marginal hires increase surplus.',
 'Opportunity cost is $30 and product value $20; the wage payment itself proves that the marginal hires increase surplus.',
 'At 60 hundreds of workers, supply is $25 and demand $15. Workers’ opportunity cost exceeds the value of their marginal output. A wage payment transfers income; it does not make a negative resource gain positive.',
 'At L=60 hundreds, S=$25 and D=$15.','Evaluate overemployment by marginal social value versus opportunity cost, not wage payments.')
p('42329','Starting from the equilibrium in the graph, employment later reaches 6,000 at $25 per hour. An analyst reports that hourly payroll rises by 50% because employment rises by 50%. Which original payroll and correction are right?',
 'Originally $80,000; payroll rises 87.5% because both wage and employment change.',
 'Originally $60,000; payroll rises 150% because both wage and employment change.',
 'Originally $80,000; payroll rises only 50% because wage changes do not affect payroll.',
 'Originally $60,000; payroll rises only 50% because wage changes do not affect payroll.',
 'The original point is 40 hundreds of workers at $20, giving $80,000 per hour. Final payroll is 6,000×25=$150,000. The increase is 70,000/80,000=87.5%, not the employment percentage alone.',
 'Original equilibrium wage $20, quantity 40 on a hundreds-of-workers axis.','Correct both axis scaling and omission of wage changes from payroll.','40*100*20=80000;6000*25=150000;(150000/80000-1)*100=87.5')
p('42331','A manager attributes the plotted equilibrium change to more workers being available at every wage. Which final outcome and diagnosis are correct?',
 '$25 and 6,000 workers; greater labor demand, not a supply expansion alone, accounts for the change.',
 '$30 and 8,000 workers; greater labor demand, not a supply expansion alone, accounts for the change.',
 '$25 and 6,000 workers; a supply expansion alone accounts for the higher wage and employment.',
 '$30 and 8,000 workers; a supply expansion alone accounts for the higher wage and employment.',
 'The new intersection is $25 and 60 hundreds of workers, up from $20 and 40 hundreds on unchanged supply. Increased demand raises both wage and employment. Increased supply alone would lower the wage.',
 'New intersection at (60 hundreds,$25); supply unchanged.','Compare competing explanations of higher wage and employment.')
p('42334','A sorting system raises marginal product 25% at every employment level while the competitive output price falls 10%. Compare the direction of the resulting VMP change with the demand shift shown. Which plotted change at the old wage and assessment are correct?',
 'Demand at $20 rises from 4,000 to 8,000 workers; the hypothetical 12.5% VMP increase supports the same shift direction.',
 'Demand at $20 rises from 4,000 to 6,000 workers; the hypothetical 12.5% VMP increase supports the same shift direction.',
 'Demand at $20 rises from 4,000 to 8,000 workers; VMP falls 10% because output price alone determines it.',
 'Demand at $20 rises from 4,000 to 6,000 workers; VMP falls 10% because output price alone determines it.',
 'At $20, DL0 gives 40 hundreds and DL1 gives 80 hundreds. Separately, VMP=P×MP changes by 0.90×1.25=1.125, a 12.5% increase. This supports the plotted direction, not necessarily its particular magnitude.',
 'At $20, DL0=40 hundreds, DL1=80 hundreds.','Combine productivity and output-price effects and distinguish agreement in direction from calibration of a shift.','1.25*.90=1.125;80*100=8000')
p('42337','A report says, “The higher wage caused labor demand to increase.” Which new wage and causal explanation match the graph?',
 '$25; a demand determinant changed first, and the resulting wage rise induces movement along labor supply.',
 '$30; a demand determinant changed first, and the resulting wage rise induces movement along labor supply.',
 '$25; the wage rise itself shifts labor demand right.',
 '$30; the wage rise itself shifts labor demand right.',
 'DL1 intersects unchanged supply at $25 and 6,000 workers. Productivity or output demand can shift labor demand; the wage responds and moves quantity supplied along the supply curve.',
 'DL1–SL intersection at $25.','Separate the cause of a demand shift from the resulting movement along supply.')
p('42343','A disease lowers each orchard worker’s marginal product 20%, while fruit prices rise 25%. Assume competitive output pricing and uniform percentage effects. Which plotted final employment and judgment about whether these two changes produce the depicted shift are correct?',
 'The graph ends at 2,000 workers; the two changes alone leave VMP unchanged and do not produce that demand shift.',
 'The graph ends at 3,000 workers; the two changes alone leave VMP unchanged and do not produce that demand shift.',
 'The graph ends at 2,000 workers; the productivity loss necessarily produces that shift regardless of fruit prices.',
 'The graph ends at 3,000 workers; the productivity loss necessarily produces that shift regardless of fruit prices.',
 'The solid demand curve intersects supply at 20 hundreds, or 2,000 workers. But the hypothetical combined change multiplies VMP by 0.80×1.25=1. A productivity effect alone cannot establish the net derived-demand shift.',
 'Solid demand–supply intersection at 20 hundreds.','Diagnose offsetting product-price and productivity effects.','20*100=2000;.80*1.25=1')
p('42341','The graph shows a productivity loss at an unchanged output price. A later output-price increase raises marginal revenue product by an unspecified amount. Which initial employment loss and conclusion about the later outcome are justified?',
 'A loss of 2,000 workers; final employment relative to its original level remains uncertain.',
 'A loss of 1,000 workers; final employment relative to its original level remains uncertain.',
 'A loss of 2,000 workers; any output-price increase must exactly restore the original employment.',
 'A loss of 1,000 workers; any output-price increase must exactly restore the original employment.',
 'Employment falls from 40 to 20 hundreds, a loss of 2,000. A higher output price offsets some of the derived-demand decline, but without its magnitude the final level relative to the original 4,000 is undetermined.',
 'Initial and new quantities 40 and 20 hundreds.','Separate a measured initial effect from an ambiguous combined result.','(40-20)*100=2000')
p('42353','Expanded nursing programs cause the supply shift shown. Hospitals then face increased demand for care, of unknown size. Which wage after the first shift and final comparison with the original equilibrium are justified?',
 'The first shift lowers wage to $15; after both shifts employment is higher, but the final wage relative to the original is ambiguous.',
 'The first shift lowers wage to $10; after both shifts employment is higher, but the final wage relative to the original is ambiguous.',
 'The first shift lowers wage to $15; after both shifts employment is higher and the final wage must remain below the original.',
 'The first shift lowers wage to $10; after both shifts employment is higher and the final wage must remain below the original.',
 'SL1 intersects demand at $15 and 6,000 workers. Added labor supply and later added labor demand both increase employment. Their wage effects oppose each other, so the final wage relative to the original $20 depends on shift sizes.',
 'SL1–DL intersection at $15, 60 hundreds.','Combine a graph-specific first change with ambiguity under opposing wage effects.')
p('42365','A report calls all excess job seekers at the plotted wage floor “jobs lost.” Which figures and correction distinguish these measures?',
 'Jobs fall by 2,000; excess labor supply is 4,000 because the higher wage also attracts additional job seekers.',
 'Jobs fall by 1,000; excess labor supply is 2,000 because the higher wage also attracts additional job seekers.',
 'Jobs fall by 2,000; excess labor supply is 4,000, and both figures measure the same decline in employment.',
 'Jobs fall by 1,000; excess labor supply is 2,000, and both figures measure the same decline in employment.',
 'Competitive employment is 40 hundreds. At the $20 floor, demand is 20 hundreds and supply 60 hundreds. Jobs lost=(40−20)×100=2,000; excess supply=(60−20)×100=4,000. The second includes new job seekers.',
 'Equilibrium L=40; at floor Ld=20,Ls=60, all in hundreds.','Distinguish reduced employment from excess labor supplied.','(40-20)*100=2000;(60-20)*100=4000')
p('42324','Which wage and employment pair is the intersection shown, and what does that intersection represent?',
 '$20 and 4,000 workers; aggregate labor demanded equals aggregate labor supplied.',
 '$25 and 6,000 workers; aggregate labor demanded equals aggregate labor supplied.',
 '$20 and 4,000 workers; this identifies a single firm’s hiring even without its marginal-product schedule.',
 '$25 and 6,000 workers; this identifies a single firm’s hiring even without its marginal-product schedule.',
 'The intersection is $20 and 40 hundreds of workers. This is a market supply-and-demand graph, so it determines total employment and the market wage, not a particular employer’s hiring.',
 'Market intersection at (40 hundreds,$20).','Interpret market equilibrium rather than a single-firm hiring decision.')
p('42335','Customer demand for next-day delivery rises. Which new equilibrium in the graph and explanation are consistent with that event?',
 '$25 and 6,000 workers; more valuable warehouse output raises derived labor demand.',
 '$30 and 8,000 workers; more valuable warehouse output raises derived labor demand.',
 '$25 and 6,000 workers; the resulting higher wage is itself what shifts labor demand.',
 '$30 and 8,000 workers; the resulting higher wage is itself what shifts labor demand.',
 'DL1 intersects supply at $25 and 60 hundreds. Labor demand derives from the output workers produce: stronger demand for delivery can raise their marginal product value. Wage is a resulting price, not the demand shifter.',
 'DL1 equilibrium (60 hundreds,$25).','Explain derived demand and distinguish a demand determinant from its wage outcome.')
p('42342','Apple prices fall, with worker productivity unchanged. Which final equilibrium and demand interpretation match the graph?',
 '$15 and 2,000 workers; lower VMP shifts labor demand left.',
 '$10 and 4,000 workers; lower VMP shifts labor demand left.',
 '$15 and 2,000 workers; lower VMP shifts labor supply right.',
 '$10 and 4,000 workers; lower VMP shifts labor supply right.',
 'DL1 intersects supply at $15 and 20 hundreds. A lower output price lowers VMP=P×MP, reducing demand for labor at each wage.',
 'Solid DL1 intersection at (20 hundreds,$15).','Connect an output-price decline to derived labor demand.')
p('42346','At $15 per hour, compare the two demand curves in the graph. Which hiring change and distinction between a shift and a movement are correct?',
 'Demand falls from 6,000 to 2,000 workers; a change at the same wage is a demand shift.',
 'Demand falls from 5,000 to 1,000 workers; a change at the same wage is a demand shift.',
 'Demand falls from 6,000 to 2,000 workers; the unchanged wage itself causes movement along one demand curve.',
 'Demand falls from 5,000 to 1,000 workers; the unchanged wage itself causes movement along one demand curve.',
 'At $15, DL0 gives 60 hundreds and DL1 gives 20 hundreds. Different hiring at a common wage identifies a shifted demand schedule; a wage change alone moves along one schedule.',
 'DL0($15)=60 hundreds; DL1($15)=20 hundreds.','Distinguish a change in demand from a wage-induced change in quantity demanded.')
for i,graphq,alt,kind,why in [
 ('42344',4000,2000,'surplus','Labor supplied exceeds labor demanded on the new demand curve, putting downward pressure on wage.'),
 ('42352',4000,2000,'surplus','Labor supplied on the new supply curve exceeds labor demanded, putting downward pressure on wage.'),
 ('42360',4000,2000,'shortage','Labor demanded exceeds labor supplied on the new supply curve, putting upward pressure on wage.')]:
 wrong='shortage' if kind=='surplus' else 'surplus';direction='downward' if kind=='surplus' else 'upward';opposite='upward' if direction=='downward' else 'downward'
 p(i,'After the plotted shift, suppose the wage initially stays at its old $20 level. Which imbalance and wage pressure follow?',
 f'A {graphq:,}-worker {kind}, putting {direction} pressure on wage.',f'A {alt:,}-worker {kind}, putting {direction} pressure on wage.',
 f'A {graphq:,}-worker {wrong}, putting {opposite} pressure on wage.',f'A {alt:,}-worker {wrong}, putting {opposite} pressure on wage.',
 ('At $20, supply is 40 hundreds and new demand is zero. ' if i=='42344' else 'At $20, new supply is 80 hundreds and demand is 40 hundreds. ' if i=='42352' else 'At $20, demand is 40 hundreds and new supply is zero. ')+why,
 'At $20, quantities differ by 40 hundreds; '+('supply exceeds demand.' if kind=='surplus' else 'demand exceeds supply.'),'Infer disequilibrium and wage adjustment from quantities offered and wanted.','40*100=4000')
for i,increase,context in [('42350',True,'A licensing reform lets more trained healthcare workers enter the occupation.'),('42358',False,'Many construction workers leave the region.'),('42361',False,'Better opportunities in another industry draw workers away from construction.'),('42363',False,'A student says the new supply curve merely reflects a wage change.')]:
 old,new=(4000,8000) if increase else (4000,0);altold,altnew=(3000,7000) if increase else (5000,1000)
 p(i,context+' At $20, which plotted quantities and interpretation are correct?',
 f'Supply changes from {old:,} to {new:,} workers; availability changes at a given wage, shifting supply.',
 f'Supply changes from {altold:,} to {altnew:,} workers; availability changes at a given wage, shifting supply.',
 f'Supply changes from {old:,} to {new:,} workers; this is movement along a fixed supply curve caused solely by wage.',
 f'Supply changes from {altold:,} to {altnew:,} workers; this is movement along a fixed supply curve caused solely by wage.',
 f'At $20, SL0 gives {old:,} workers and SL1 gives {new:,}. '+('Easier entry expands' if increase else 'Worker exit reduces')+' the quantity available at each wage. A wage change by itself moves along a supply curve rather than relocating it.',
 f'At $20, SL0={old/100:g},SL1={new/100:g}, in hundreds.','Distinguish a change in occupational availability or outside options from movement along supply.')
for i,increase in [('42349',True),('42357',False)]:
 w,l,wa,la=(15,6000,10,8000) if increase else (25,2000,30,1000)
 p(i,'Refer to the graph. Which new wage and employment pair follows the supply shift, and what happens on the unchanged labor-demand curve?',
 f'${w} and {l:,} workers; the market moves along labor demand.',f'${wa} and {la:,} workers; the market moves along labor demand.',
 f'${w} and {l:,} workers; the wage change itself shifts labor demand.',f'${wa} and {la:,} workers; the wage change itself shifts labor demand.',
 f'The new intersection is ${w} and {l//100} hundreds of workers. Supply shifts, while the wage change produces movement along unchanged demand. It does not itself shift labor demand.',
 f'SL1–DL intersection at ({l//100} hundreds,${w}).','Link the supply shift to equilibrium and movement along demand.')
p('42369','Compared with competition in the graph, what wage and employment prevail under the minimum wage, and which side of the market limits actual hiring?',
 '$20 and 2,000 workers; firms’ demand limits employment.','$25 and 1,000 workers; firms’ demand limits employment.',
 '$20 and 2,000 workers; the number wanting work determines employment regardless of firms’ demand.','$25 and 1,000 workers; the number wanting work determines employment regardless of firms’ demand.',
 'The $20 floor is above the $15 equilibrium. At $20 firms demand 20 hundreds, so 2,000 are employed despite 6,000 willing workers. Actual trade is limited by the short side, labor demand.',
 'Floor $20, Ld=20 hundreds; equilibrium wage $15.','Determine employment under a binding wage floor rather than treating supply as jobs.')
p('42378','Refer to the monopsony graph. How much lower are wage and employment than under competition, and what hiring rule accounts for the difference?',
 '$5 and 2,000 workers lower; monopsony equates MFC with MRP, then pays the supply-curve wage.',
 '$10 and 4,000 workers lower; monopsony equates MFC with MRP, then pays the supply-curve wage.',
 '$5 and 2,000 workers lower; monopsony equates the wage itself with MRP to choose employment.',
 '$10 and 4,000 workers lower; monopsony equates the wage itself with MRP to choose employment.',
 'MFC=MRP at 40 hundreds, with wage $20 on supply. Competition has supply=MRP at 60 hundreds and $25. Monopsony restricts hiring because marginal factor cost exceeds the wage.',
 'MFC–MRP Q=40, S(40)=$20; S–MRP Q=60,w=$25.','Compare monopsony and competition using the distinct hiring and wage rules.','25-20=5;(60-40)*100=2000')
p('42327','At the market equilibrium shown, what does one additional employed worker receive per hour, and why?',
 '$20; competitive workers receive the common market wage.','$25; competitive workers receive the common market wage.',
 '$20; the demand intercept is the payment every worker receives.','$25; the demand intercept is the payment every worker receives.',
 'Supply and demand intersect at wage $20. A worker in this competitive market receives that common factor price, not the demand intercept or the total value of output.',
 'Equilibrium wage $20; demand intercept $30.','Interpret the competitive factor price paid to a worker.')
p('42338','The demand curves differ at the old wage, but equilibrium employment does not increase by that full horizontal distance. Which actual employment increase and explanation are correct?',
 '2,000 workers; the wage rises as the market moves along labor supply.',
 '1,000 workers; the wage rises as the market moves along labor supply.',
 '2,000 workers; the higher wage itself shifts labor supply left.',
 '1,000 workers; the higher wage itself shifts labor supply left.',
 'Equilibrium employment rises from 40 to 60 hundreds, or 2,000. At $20 the demand shift is 40 hundreds, but wage bidding raises pay to $25, inducing movement up supply and limiting the equilibrium quantity change.',
 'Old/new equilibria L=40/60 hundreds; demand at old wage shifts 40→80.','Explain why an equilibrium response differs from a horizontal demand shift.','(60-40)*100=2000')
p('42355','Which two equilibrium employment levels in the graph support interpreting the adjustment as movement along unchanged labor demand?',
 '4,000 and 6,000; both equilibria lie on the same demand schedule.',
 '3,000 and 5,000; both equilibria lie on the same demand schedule.',
 '4,000 and 6,000; a lower wage by itself creates a different demand schedule.',
 '3,000 and 5,000; a lower wage by itself creates a different demand schedule.',
 'The equilibria at 40 and 60 hundreds both lie on DL. Supply changes, while the wage decline produces a change in quantity demanded along that single demand curve.',
 'DL passes through (40,$20) and (60,$15), quantities in hundreds.','Identify movement along an unchanged demand schedule.')
p('42364','Why is the minimum wage in the graph binding, and how far above the competitive wage is it?',
 '$5 above it; the legal lower bound prevents adjustment to the market-clearing wage.',
 '$10 above it; the legal lower bound prevents adjustment to the market-clearing wage.',
 '$5 above it; a lower bound binds because any positive minimum wage fixes all wages.',
 '$10 above it; a lower bound binds because any positive minimum wage fixes all wages.',
 'The minimum is $20 and equilibrium is $15, a $5 gap. A floor above equilibrium rules out the wage that would clear the market; positivity alone does not make it binding.',
 'Minimum $20; supply-demand intersection $15.','Apply the binding-floor criterion.','20-15=5')
for i in ['42371','42373']:
 p(i,'Refer to the minimum-wage graph. Which gap between the market-clearing wage and the legal minimum, and policy conclusion, are correct?',
 '$5; the floor is nonbinding, so it does not create a labor surplus.',
 '$10; the floor is nonbinding, so it does not create a labor surplus.',
 '$5; a legal minimum requires pay to fall to that minimum and creates a labor surplus.',
 '$10; a legal minimum requires pay to fall to that minimum and creates a labor surplus.',
 'Equilibrium is $15 while the legal minimum is $10. Paying the market wage already complies with the floor. The $5 gap therefore creates neither a forced wage reduction nor policy-induced excess supply.',
 'Equilibrium wage $15; floor $10.','Recognize that a nonbinding minimum is a lower bound, not a mandated wage.','15-10=5')
p('42376','After using the graph to find the monopsonist’s profit-maximizing employment, what wage does it pay, and where is that wage read?',
 '$20 per hour, from labor supply at the chosen employment.','$15 per hour, from labor supply at the chosen employment.',
 '$20 per hour, from MFC at the chosen employment.','$15 per hour, from MFC at the chosen employment.',
 'MFC intersects MRP at 40 hundreds of nurses. At that employment supply is $20, while MFC is $30. The wage comes from supply, not the marginal expenditure on the last hire.',
 'MFC=MRP at L=40; S(40)=$20.','Separate the wage paid from marginal factor cost.')
p('42375','Which employment and decision rule identify the monopsonist’s hiring in the graph?',
 '4,000 nurses; equate marginal factor cost with marginal revenue product.',
 '3,000 nurses; equate marginal factor cost with marginal revenue product.',
 '4,000 nurses; equate the wage on supply with marginal revenue product.',
 '3,000 nurses; equate the wage on supply with marginal revenue product.',
 'MFC and MRP intersect at 40 hundreds, or 4,000 nurses. A monopsonist compares the extra hiring expenditure with the extra revenue generated; supply=MRP is the competitive benchmark instead.',
 'MFC–MRP intersection at 40 hundreds.','Apply the monopsony marginal hiring rule.')
p('42377','A proposal expands hiring from the monopsony outcome toward the competitive outcome in the graph. Which employment increase and welfare argument are correct?',
 '2,000 additional nurses; their marginal product value exceeds opportunity cost, while higher wages are transfers.',
 '1,000 additional nurses; their marginal product value exceeds opportunity cost, while higher wages are transfers.',
 '2,000 additional nurses; the larger wage payments themselves are the extra real resource gains.',
 '1,000 additional nurses; the larger wage payments themselves are the extra real resource gains.',
 'MFC=MRP gives monopsony employment of 40 hundreds; supply=MRP gives 60 hundreds. In between, MRP exceeds the opportunity cost on supply. The additional 2,000 hires recover gains from trade; wage transfers alone do not measure those gains.',
 'MFC–MRP quantity 40; S–MRP quantity 60; S below MRP between them.','Evaluate monopsony employment restriction and separate transfers from real gains.','(60-40)*100=2000')
for i in ['42381','42383']:
 p(i,'Both labor demand and supply increase in the graph. Which final wage and interpretation of the combined shifts are justified?',
 '$20; the wage effects offset in this figure, but simultaneous increases need not always leave wages unchanged.',
 '$25; the wage effects offset in this figure, but simultaneous increases need not always leave wages unchanged.',
 '$20; simultaneous increases must always leave wages unchanged, regardless of their sizes.',
 '$25; simultaneous increases must always leave wages unchanged, regardless of their sizes.',
 'The old curves meet at $20 and 40 hundreds; the new curves meet at $20 and 80 hundreds. Here opposing wage effects offset exactly. In general their relative magnitudes determine the wage change, while both increases raise employment.',
 'Both intersections at wage $20; employment 40→80 hundreds.','Separate a graph-specific comparative-static result from a generally ambiguous wage effect.')
p('40032','A student claims that the minimum wage shown fixes employment at the quantity demanded and creates a worker surplus. Which equilibrium and diagnosis correctly distinguish this floor from a ceiling at the same level?',
 '$12 and 200 thousand workers remain the equilibrium; the floor is nonbinding, while a ceiling at $6 would sustain a 200-thousand-worker shortage.',
 '$10 and 150 thousand workers remain the equilibrium; the floor is nonbinding, while a ceiling at $6 would sustain a 100-thousand-worker shortage.',
 '$12 and 200 thousand workers remain the equilibrium; the floor is nonbinding, while a ceiling at $6 would sustain a 200-thousand-worker surplus.',
 '$10 and 150 thousand workers remain the equilibrium; the floor is nonbinding, while a ceiling at $6 would sustain a 100-thousand-worker surplus.',
 'The graph’s equilibrium is $12 and 200 thousand workers, above the $6 minimum. At $6, Qd=300 thousand and Qs=100 thousand: a shortage of 200 thousand. A ceiling could hold wages there, but a floor permits the equilibrium wage.',
 'Equilibrium ($12,200 thousand); floor $6, Qd=300,Qs=100 thousand.','Diagnose a nonbinding floor and distinguish a hypothetical ceiling shortage from a surplus.','300-100=200 thousand')
p('PG1-SUP-L-001','A producer claims that the move from A to B proves that supply increased. Which quantity change and diagnosis are correct?',
 'An increase of 125 thousand units; this is a change in quantity supplied along the existing curve.',
 'An increase of 100 thousand units; this is a change in quantity supplied along the existing curve.',
 'An increase of 125 thousand units; any rise in quantity is an increase in the whole supply schedule.',
 'An increase of 100 thousand units; any rise in quantity is an increase in the whole supply schedule.',
 'A is at 100 thousand units and B at 225 thousand, an increase of 125 thousand. Both lie on the same supply curve. The higher own price changes quantity supplied; an increase in supply would shift the entire schedule.',
 'A Q=100 thousand, B Q=225 thousand on one supply curve.','Diagnose movement along supply rather than a change in supply.','225-100=125 thousand')
save();print('Graph proposals prepared:',len(R))
